<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 130 0 R
/T (In the structure design, all details of a patient stay together in one record. To sort by age we swap one whole structure, so a name can never be separated from its age. A patient can be passed to a function with one pointer instead of five values. New data, like a blood group, is added as one new member and the old code still works. Nested structures keep date and blood pressure clear, and the date can be compared easily. A count check stops overflow.)
/E (In the structure design, all details of a patient stay together in one record. To sort by age we swap one whole structure, so a name can never be separated from its age. A patient can be passed to a function with one pointer instead of five values. New data, like a blood group, is added as one new member and the old code still works. Nested structures keep date and blood pressure clear, and the date can be compared easily. A count check stops overflow.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
