/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 134 0 R
/T (A union keeps all its members at the same address, so its size is the size of the biggest member. Only one member can be valid at a time. A tagged union is a structure with an extra field \(an enum tag\) that remembers which member is valid.)
/E (A union keeps all its members at the same address, so its size is the size of the biggest member. Only one member can be valid at a time. A tagged union is a structure with an extra field \(an enum tag\) that remembers which member is valid.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 134 0 R
/T (Alignment and padding: the compiler places each member at an address that suits its type, and makes the total size a multiple of the largest alignment. Extra bytes added for this are called padding.)
