532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 135 0 R
/T (Three separate arrays \(one per type\): each packet needs only its own size \(4, 4 or 8 bytes\), but the program must also keep the order of arrival \(an extra index or tag per packet\), so the saving is lost, and reading a packet in arrival order is harder. malloc with a void pointer per packet: each packet needs an 8-byte pointer, plus the tag, plus the memory manager's own bookkeeping \(usually 16 to 32 bytes per allocation\), so it is much bigger than 12 bytes. It is also slower, because the pointer has to be followed, and memory can be fragmented. Tagged union: reading a packet is O\(1\) \(check the tag, read one member\) and the cost is a fixed 12 bytes per packet, in one continuous array, so it is the best design here.)
/E (Three separate arrays \(one per type\): each packet needs only its own size \(4, 4 or 8 bytes\), but the program must also keep the order of arrival \(an extra index or tag per packet\), so the saving is lost, and reading a packet in arrival order is harder. malloc with a void pointer per packet: each packet needs an 8-byte pointer, plus the tag, plus the memory manager's own bookkeeping \(usually 16 to 32 bytes per allocation\), so it is much bigger than 12 bytes. It is also slower, because the pointer has to be followed, and memory can be fragmented. Tagged union: reading a packet is O\(1\) \(check the tag, read one member\) and the cost is a fixed 12 bytes per packet, in one continuous array, so it is the best design here.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [39]
>>
endobj
