/T (The program above demonstrates: normal sales \(Samosa, Juice, Combo\), a sale that exceeds stock \(50 biscuits, only 20 available, so it was refused\), an expiring item \(Biscuits expire tomorrow, 1 day left\) and the summary sorted by revenue \(Juice 100, Combo 80, Samosa 60, Biscuits 0, total 240\). The combo sale does not reduce the stock of its parts, and days_of\(\) ignores the 100/400-year leap rules; these are simplifications.)
/E (The program above demonstrates: normal sales \(Samosa, Juice, Combo\), a sale that exceeds stock \(50 biscuits, only 20 available, so it was refused\), an expiring item \(Biscuits expire tomorrow, 1 day left\) and the summary sorted by revenue \(Juice 100, Combo 80, Samosa 60, Biscuits 0, total 240\). The combo sale does not reduce the stock of its parts, and days_of\(\) ignores the 100/400-year leap rules; these are simplifications.)
