/Title (When are parallel arrays still better? When a program works on only one field for all patients, for example finding the average age. The ages are stored together in memory, so scanning is fast and no padding is wasted. Parallel arrays are also good when a field is added only for a few operations.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1773 0 R
/Next 1788 0 R
>>
endobj
1781 0 obj
<<
/Title (Space: parallel arrays use 46 bytes per patient = 4,600 bytes for 100 patients, with no padding. The structure array uses 56 bytes per patient = 5,600 bytes. The extra bytes are the 1 extra byte for '\\0' in phone and 1 padding byte \(the structure's size is a multiple of 4 because of alignment\). The small extra space is a good price for safer code.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1773 0 R
/Prev 1788 0 R
>>
endobj
