1956 0 obj
<<
/Title (This also works \(output below is from t4_value.c, also run\). Time: the structure is copied into the function and copied again on return, so 2 x sizeof\(struct account\) bytes per call. For struct account this is 2 x 8 = 16 bytes, while a pointer copies only one address \(8 bytes\). Stack space: by value uses sizeof\(struct\) for the parameter plus a temporary for the returned copy; by pointer uses a single 8-byte pointer. For a small structure the difference is tiny, but for a large one, like the 56-byte Patient in Task 1, by value costs 112 bytes per call against 8 bytes. In both cases the time grows only with the structure size and not with the number of accounts, but the pointer version is O\(1\) per call and the value version is O\(sizeof\(struct\)\).)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1940 0 R
>>
endobj
